<section style="padding:72px 24px;background:#EEF2F7;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">FAQ</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(24px,3.5vw,36px);margin:0 0 32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 10px;">Is Prairie Radon Co certified by C-NRPP or approved by Health Canada?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">No. We are not a lab and hold no certification. The independent professional who tests your home or installs a system carries their own certification, and you can verify it in the C-NRPP online directory. Health Canada recommends using a C-NRPP certified professional for mitigation.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 10px;">Do I have to fix radon by law in Manitoba or Saskatchewan?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">No. The 200 Bq/m3 figure is a Health Canada guideline, and neither province requires homeowners to test or mitigate. Above 200, Health Canada recommends fixing it within a year. Below 200, it notes that no level is considered risk free.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E6E0D2;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Oswald',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#16263B;margin:0 0 10px;">Why tell me to test for three months instead of booking an install?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Because a mitigation quote without a proper result is guesswork, and short tests and digital monitors are screening only. The money help needs it too: the Lungs Matter grant asks for a C-NRPP test of 90 days or more above 200 Bq/m3. A kit costs between $50 and $100, according to the Manitoba government, and libraries in Winnipeg, Regina and Moose Jaw lend free screening monitors.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
